WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.937
Meet Floorsight, our entry for the OpenCV AI Competition.

2
00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:07.378
We help safety teams measure forklift risk from existing cameras,
check the evidence, and

3
00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:10.818
review a warning before alerting a supervisor.

4
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.065
A worker can look almost touching a forklift in a camera image,
while being metres away on

5
00:00:14.095 --> 00:00:16.680
the floor.

6
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.485
Pixel distance can create misleading alarms.

7
00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:22.176
Floorsight maps the scene onto the floor, so a reviewer gets an
estimated distance and its

8
00:00:22.206 --> 00:00:24.866
uncertainty.

9
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.005
OpenCV five handles camera geometry, motion, tracking, risk
zones, privacy filtering and

10
00:00:29.035 --> 00:00:32.520
video.

11
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.412
Separate PyTorch models detect people, forklifts and loads.

12
00:00:36.442 --> 00:00:41.405
We estimate a close pass, including stopping distance, turning
space and hidden areas.

13
00:00:41.435 --> 00:00:44.858
Real footage is processed offline; this is not live braking.

14
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.082
Here is the working dashboard replaying recorded factory footage.

15
00:00:49.112 --> 00:00:53.575
A warning links the camera view, floor map, measurements and
review history.

16
00:00:53.605 --> 00:00:57.567
The agent requests a short replay, a close-up and a second
measurement.

17
00:00:57.597 --> 00:01:01.258
A reviewer can inspect the evidence, then approve or reject the
warning.

18
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.295
The agent does more than describe a picture.

19
00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:07.762
Visual evidence changes what it asks for and recommends next.

20
00:01:07.792 --> 00:01:10.567
A tracking jump alone cannot dismiss a hazard.

21
00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:13.410
Conflicting or missing measurements stay uncertain.

22
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.165
Floor-warning alerts require human approval.

23
00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:21.138
The demonstrated planner is local; live Bedrock access remains
blocked by account settings.

24
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.203
In controlled two-camera scenes, false exit alerts fell from
eight to zero, while all eight

25
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:28.637
visible hazards still produced alerts.

26
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.380
All sixteen cases without a second camera went to human review.

27
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.935
These are executable synthetic tests with saved evidence and
traces, not a field accuracy

28
00:01:34.965 --> 00:01:37.490
claim.

29
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.874
On eighty synthetic floor warnings, hazardous rejection
recommendations fell from sixteen to

30
00:01:40.904 --> 00:01:43.657
zero.

31
00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:47.150
False candidates recommended for action fell by forty percent.

32
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.417
All cases still require review.

33
00:01:49.447 --> 00:01:51.938
We count uncertainty as actionable, so the score cannot improve
just by hiding difficult

34
00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:54.458
cases.

35
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.332
To test intervention, we built a simulated warehouse.

36
00:01:58.362 --> 00:02:02.500
Without control, the forklift contacts two people who enter its
path.

37
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.359
This lets us test feedback from vision to movement without
claiming that software can stop a

38
00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:08.218
truck in recorded video.

39
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.292
With control on, both in-path contacts were avoided in that saved
scenario.

40
00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:18.147
The cost was slower work: twenty-seven metres travelled instead
of eighty in forty seconds.

41
00:02:18.177 --> 00:02:22.902
The system cannot prevent every contact, or react to a person no
camera sees.

42
00:02:22.932 --> 00:02:25.458
Real forklift integration has not been performed.

43
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.174
The architecture connects cameras, OpenCV perception, floor-risk
estimates and the review

44
00:02:30.204 --> 00:02:34.257
agent on an AWS Graviton instance using COOL.

45
00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:37.997
S3 stores evidence, DynamoDB keeps track history, CloudWatch
records activity, and SNS

46
00:02:38.027 --> 00:02:41.737
delivers approved alerts.

47
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.146
The recorded AWS run exercised all four adapters.

48
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.691
For the COOL track, we publish the imported build, Graviton
configuration and baseline

49
00:02:48.721 --> 00:02:51.612
comparisons.

50
00:02:51.642 --> 00:02:54.609
The historical OpenCV chain improved by one point one times, but
full perception did not

51
00:02:54.639 --> 00:02:57.605
improve.

52
00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:01.822
New quality-checked experiments also found no meaningful pipeline
speedup.

53
00:03:01.852 --> 00:03:04.226
Detector inference remains the main bottleneck.

54
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.771
The public repository includes a self-contained simulator, three
hundred and six passing

55
00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:14.692
tests on both Linux and Windows, evaluation scripts, model
revisions and raw receipts.

56
00:03:14.722 --> 00:03:19.322
Terraform, bootstrap code and a deployment guide are included for
AWS.

57
00:03:19.352 --> 00:03:22.442
No private footage or credentials are needed to run the
simulator.

58
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.442
Floorsight turns existing cameras into measured, reviewable
safety evidence.

59
00:03:27.472 --> 00:03:30.069
Our contribution is the combination of floor geometry, an
evidence-driven agent and human

60
00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:32.697
control.

61
00:03:32.727 --> 00:03:37.002
The code, deployment instructions, demo and limitations are
public.

62
00:03:37.032 --> 00:03:40.882
The next step is independent labelled footage and testing with
real safety teams.

